.Dd October 14, 2026
.Dt XFT_SIGNALS 3
.Os xft
.Sh NAME
.Nm xft_signals
.Nd signal number constants
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In signals.h
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.In signals.h
defines only constants; there is no signal set type and no function in
it.
.Ss Constants
The constants live in
.In types/signal_types.h .
The mask operation values are
.Bd -literal -offset indent
XFT_BLOCK    = 0
XFT_UNBLOCK  = 1
XFT_SETMASK  = 2
.Ed
.Pp
On x86_64 and aarch64 the signal numbers are defined by the header
itself, using the Linux values:
.Dv XFT_SIGHUP ,
.Dv XFT_SIGINT ,
.Dv XFT_SIGQUIT ,
.Dv XFT_SIGILL ,
.Dv XFT_SIGTRAP ,
.Dv XFT_SIGABRT ,
.Dv XFT_SIGBUS ,
.Dv XFT_SIGFPE ,
.Dv XFT_SIGKILL ,
.Dv XFT_SIGUSR1 ,
.Dv XFT_SIGSEGV ,
.Dv XFT_SIGUSR2 ,
.Dv XFT_SIGPIPE ,
.Dv XFT_SIGALRM ,
.Dv XFT_SIGTERM ,
.Dv XFT_SIGSTKFLT ,
.Dv XFT_SIGCHLD ,
.Dv XFT_SIGCONT ,
.Dv XFT_SIGSTOP ,
.Dv XFT_SIGTSTP ,
.Dv XFT_SIGTTIN ,
.Dv XFT_SIGTTOU ,
.Dv XFT_SIGURG ,
.Dv XFT_SIGXCPU ,
.Dv XFT_SIGXFSZ ,
.Dv XFT_SIGVTALRM ,
.Dv XFT_SIGPROF ,
.Dv XFT_SIGWINCH ,
.Dv XFT_SIGIO ,
.Dv XFT_SIGPWR
and
.Dv XFT_SIGSYS .
On other Linux architectures they are aliases for the platform's own
.Dv SIG*
constants; the library still exposes the
.Ql XFT_
names so callers do not have to include
.In signal.h
themselves.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xft_os 3 ,
.Xr xft 7
.Sh CAVEATS
Windows has no signals.
On x86_64 and aarch64 Windows the Linux numbers above are defined;
on other Windows architectures the header defines nothing.
.Pp
Only the signal numbers listed above are defined by the header.
Any other signal, real-time signal, or the platform's
.Dv SIGRTMIN
through
.Dv SIGRTMAX
range, must be named numerically.
